================================================================================
METHOD — STANDING-ORDERS §7 (scapy, not ping)
================================================================================
A ping cannot express a DNS relay. Each leg sends N crafted DNS A queries from eth3 to the
DUT's VLAN-10 SVI (L2 destination e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43, source 192.168.10.50, which the query tool
ARP-answers for so the DUT can reply), and counts at BOTH ends:
  eth3 — the queries we actually emitted and the answers that came back;
  eth4 — the queries the DUT FORWARDED upstream and the upstream replies;
  the responder's own record — which upstream server was asked, for what, and when.
Each positive leg is paired with a control in the same configuration that must forward zero.
**Settle time is part of the method**: per the TFS Limitation, any DNS-relay config change —
including the DHCP client learning a server — restarts the relay process. Legs wait 20 s after
such a change. A 3 s wait produced a spurious zero early on; see "THE SETTLE TRAP".

