================================================================================
STEP 4 — the ORDERING limb (S1570.1.12 "added last"): measured on the wire, not the show
================================================================================
⚠ `show ip name-server` CANNOT answer this. It groups by type — it printed
`192.168.48.2 dynamic` above `192.168.48.9 static` both before and after a re-lease, which is
consistent with "grouped display" and with "dynamic really is first", and distinguishes
neither. Grading the limb on that output would have been a coin flip dressed as a result.

**The discriminator is which server the relay actually queries.** Two responders were run on
eth4 at once, each answering ARP for its own address and each returning a DIFFERENT A record:
    192.168.48.9  (static)         -> 10.9.9.9
    192.168.48.2  (DHCP-learned)   -> 10.2.2.2
and one query for `ck-order.test` was sent from eth3.

  leg `order`:
    .9 static : UPSTREAM QUERIES RECEIVED: 0   answered 0   **arp answered 0**
    .2 dynamic: UPSTREAM QUERIES RECEIVED: 1   answered 1   arp answered 1
        t=26.8217  ck-order.test  from 192.168.48.100
    tcpdump eth4:
        17:01:54.804064 IP 192.168.48.100.50058 > 192.168.48.2.53: A? ck-order.test.
        17:01:54.883288 IP 192.168.48.2.53 > 192.168.48.100.50058: 1/0/0 A 10.2.2.2
    client received: ck-order.test -> 10.2.2.2

  The DUT queried ONLY the DHCP-learned server. It did not even ARP for the static one.

**Control, to rule out "the static server was unreachable" rather than "not preferred".**
Same bench, same addressing; only the DHCP-learned entry is removed, by re-leasing from a DHCP
server that offers NO option 6:
    show ip name-server -> Currently learned name-servers:
                           192.168.48.9 static        (only)
    show ip interface brief -> vlan48 192.168.48.100/24     (still addressed)
  leg `ctl2`, after a 20 s settle, 6 queries:
    .9 static : UPSTREAM QUERIES RECEIVED: 4   answered 4   arp answered 1
    tcpdump eth4:
        17:05:43.998660 ARP, Request who-has 192.168.48.9 tell 192.168.48.100
        17:05:44.031007 ARP, Reply 192.168.48.9 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f
        17:05:44.031306 IP 192.168.48.100.49266 > 192.168.48.9.53: A? ck-a.test.
        17:05:44.119328 IP 192.168.48.9.53 > 192.168.48.100.49266: 1/0/0 A 10.9.9.9
  ⇒ The static server at .9 is perfectly reachable and is used when it is the only one.

**Conclusion of the pair:** with BOTH a static and a DHCP-learned server configured, this DUT
sends its query to the **DHCP-learned** server and never consults the static one, although the
static one is demonstrably usable. Recorded as OBSERVATION O-6 below rather than graded as a
failure, because the TFS wording ("added last of configured name-server list") describes LIST
POSITION, and a resolver is not obliged to walk its list in order — dnsmasq may select a server
by responsiveness or by most-recent-update. What the measurement establishes is the externally
visible behaviour; what it cannot establish is the internal list order the spec talks about.
That distinction is the finding.

================================================================================
THE CACHE LIMB (S1570.1.03) — INCONCLUSIVE, recorded with its reason, NOT graded
================================================================================
    awplus(config)# ip dns forwarding cache size 100 time 60
    show ip dns forwarding -> DNS Cache: enabled   Size: 100   Max-TTL: 60 second(s)
    (the running-config renders this as `ip dns forwarding cache size 100 timeout 60`)

  leg `cache`: the SAME name `ck-cache.test` queried twice, 5.3 s apart, well inside the 60 s
  TTL, with the upstream responder returning ttl 60 both times:
    round 1: answered 10.9.9.9
    round 2: answered 10.9.9.9
    upstream queries for ck-cache.test over BOTH rounds: **2**
        17:10:24.518819 IP 192.168.48.100.30837 > 192.168.48.9.53: A? ck-cache.test.
        17:10:29.818847 IP 192.168.48.100.40305 > 192.168.48.9.53: A? ck-cache.test.

  A cache hit should have made the second upstream query absent. It was not absent.

  **I am NOT grading this as a failure, for a specific reason I could not resolve on the
  bench.** The TFS describes two distinct processes (§"Target Feature List"): a *Name Resolver
  Process* for local applications and a *DNS Relay Process* for forwarding. S1570.1.03 reads
  "Caches DNS **name resolve** result" and S1570.1.11 "Configures **name resolve result** cache
  size" — wording that plausibly scopes the cache to the name-resolver path (the device's own
  lookups) rather than to relayed third-party queries. If that reading is right, forwarding
  every relayed query upstream is correct behaviour and my test premise was simply wrong — the
  same class of mistake as the `agent-option checking` direction error in Group B, which the
  documentation caught before it became a verdict. I found no text that settles it either way,
  and nothing on this bench can distinguish "the relay path is not cached by design" from "the
  cache is not working". **Limb recorded UNMEASURED with this reason**; it does not affect the
  case verdict, which rests on the forwarding limbs that were measured cleanly at both ends.
  Offered to the Test Engineer as the one item in this campaign that would benefit from a
  developer's answer rather than another bench run.

================================================================================
THE SETTLE TRAP — a spurious zero, and how it was caught rather than reported
================================================================================
The first run of the static-only control forwarded NOTHING: zero upstream queries, zero ARPs,
no answer. Read naively that is "the DUT cannot use a statically configured DNS server" — a
serious false finding. It was a **settle-time artefact of the TFS Limitation**: the preceding
`no ip address dhcp` / `ip address dhcp` is a DHCP-client DNS update, which restarts the relay
process and clears its sessions. I had waited ~3 s. Re-run after a 20 s settle, the identical
configuration forwarded 4 of 4 queries and ARPed for the server first (quoted in STEP 4).
Every graded leg in this log therefore waits 20 s after any DNS-relay or DHCP-client change.
This is the TFS's own documented limitation biting exactly as written, and it is the reason the
method section treats settle time as part of the measurement rather than as hygiene.

Also noted and not over-read: an early multi-round leg sent 6 queries and received 4 answers,
with 4 reaching the upstream responder. Two queries for names already in flight produced no
separate upstream query. Not investigated, and deliberately NOT used as cache evidence — the
graded legs all use DISTINCT names in a single round precisely so that in-flight duplicate
suppression cannot contaminate a count.

