================================================================================
VERDICT: PASS -- the DUT relays DNS correctly as a DHCP client. With `ip dns forwarding`
enabled it forwarded 5 of 5 crafted queries to the upstream server from its own DHCP-leased
address and returned all 5 correct answers to the querying host, counted independently at both
NICs; with forwarding disabled the same 5 queries were demonstrably on the wire and 0 were
forwarded. A DNS server learned by the DHCP client is imported, tagged `dynamic (vlan48)`, and
is genuinely used. All settings survive a reboot, proven on the wire. The cache limb is
recorded UNMEASURED with its reason -- the TFS wording plausibly scopes the cache to the
name-resolver path rather than the relay path, and nothing on this bench can separate that from
a cache fault. One observation, O-6: a DHCP-learned server is queried in preference to a
statically configured one, which does not obviously match S1570.1.12's "added last".
